// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row str_annot_loses_static_for_dyn_fn (tier 3, `refuses`). A
// `str` TYPE ANNOTATION ON A STRING-LITERAL BINDING LOSES ITS `'static` REGION,
// SO A CLOSURE CAPTURING IT WILL NOT COERCE TO `dyn Fn`.
//
// `let s: str = "abc";` then `Box::new(move || s.len()) : Box<dyn Fn() -> i64>`
// is refused:
//
//   error [fn mk]: coercion to `dyn Fn` requires `|| -> i64: 'static` —
//   lifetime `'_` may not live long enough (object lifetime bound)
//
// THE SAME PROGRAM WITHOUT THE ANNOTATION COMPILES AND RUNS CORRECTLY. That is
// the whole finding: `let s = "abc";` mints the literal's `'static` region on
// the binding, and writing the type out replaces it with the empty spelling,
// which the object-lifetime bound then reads as "may not live long enough". A
// type ascription that names the SAME type must not change what the program
// means.
//
// Legal Rust: a `&'static str` in a `move` closure makes the closure `'static`,
// and `Box::new(move || s.len()) as Box<dyn Fn() -> i64>` is accepted.
//
// PAIR, one token apart, both measured 2026-09-07 on build febd1aa6e49ae30c 43
// (closure lattice cells e_fat_boxed_1/2/3 against i_fat_boxed_noannot_1/2/3):
// with the annotation, refused at all three capture counts; without it,
// accepted at all three. The non-boxed spelling `let s: str = "abc"; let f =
// move || s.len(); f();` is ACCEPTED with the annotation (cells e_fat_1/2/3),
// so the annotation is not itself rejected — only the `dyn Fn` object-lifetime
// bound reads the lost region.
//
// ⚠ THE ACCEPTED HALF IS NOT SOUND EITHER, AND IT IS A DIFFERENT ROW: the
// unannotated spelling compiles and then overflows its environment
// (boxed_move_closure_fat_capture_env_overflow). Repairing THIS row must not be
// scored against that one.
package str_annot_loses_static_for_dyn_fn;

use logos.mem.boxed;

fn mk() -> Box<dyn Fn() -> i64> {
    let s: str = "abc";
    return Box::new(move || -> i64 { return s.len() as i64; });
}

fn main() -> i32 {
    let f: Box<dyn Fn() -> i64> = mk();
    if f() != 3i64 { return 1i32; }
    return 0i32;
}
